WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.567
MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

2
00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:08.733
One open standard for plugging AI into tools and data, with no custom glue.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:14.387
MCP is the open standard for connecting AI to tools and data.

4
00:00:14.387 --> 00:00:17.527
Anthropic created it in twenty twenty-four,

5
00:00:17.527 --> 00:00:22.200
and the Linux Foundation's Agentic AI Foundation now governs it.

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.100
A server exposes tools, which are functions the model can call.

7
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.533
Resources, which are data it can read.

8
00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:31.497
And prompts, which are reusable templates.

9
00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:36.000
Any client, like Claude Desktop or your own app, plugs into any server.

10
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.067
Here is the math behind the USB-C analogy.

11
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:43.367
Say you have five apps and eight data sources.

12
00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:46.888
Without a standard, that is forty custom integrations.

13
00:00:46.888 --> 00:00:52.693
With MCP, each app implements the client once and each source implements the server once.

14
00:00:52.693 --> 00:00:53.801
That is thirteen.

15
00:00:53.801 --> 00:00:56.867
This economics is why the ecosystem snowballed.

16
00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:00.167
Now what happens when a client connects.

17
00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:01.933
The client starts the session.

18
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:06.867
First comes the initialize handshake, negotiating version and capabilities.

19
00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:10.433
Then the client discovers what is on offer with tools list.

20
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:12.533
And it calls a tool with tools call.

21
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:17.833
Here is the same thing as simplified JSON-RPC messages.

22
00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:22.300
The client sends initialize, and the server answers with what it supports.

23
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.800
Then tools list returns the available tools, such as close approaches.

24
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.933
And tools call runs one, returning its content.

25
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.733
Here is a skill worth having.

26
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.867
Install any MCP server into Claude Code.

27
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:40.800
On startup the client spawned the server, ran the handshake, and called tools list.

28
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:45.845
Those schemas joined the model's tool set, which is why the tools just appeared.

29
00:01:45.845 --> 00:01:49.167
Explaining that, not just using it, is the scarce skill.

30
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:51.276
Let's recap.

31
00:01:51.276 --> 00:01:54.533
MCP is an open standard for tools and data.

32
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:57.800
Servers expose tools, resources, and prompts.

33
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.433
It turns M times N integrations into M plus N.

34
00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:04.425
The flow is handshake, list, then call.

35
00:02:04.425 --> 00:02:06.633
Next up: building an MCP server.
